  number={},
  pages={200-205},
  abstract={With the widespread application of Large Language Models (LLMs), prompts have become the core interface for usermodel interaction, and their security directly impacts the compliance and reliability of generated content. However, malicious users may craft misleading, evasive, or adversarial prompts to induce models to generate inappropriate content. Traditional detection methods based on rules or post-hoc filtering struggle to handle semantically complex and variably formed attacks. To address this, this paper proposes a Prompt-aware Adaptive Multi-task BERT (PAMBert) method for prompt violation detection. This method dynamically integrates multiscale local and global features through an adaptive hierarchical pooling mechanism, introduces task-difficulty-aware dynamic loss weighting to alleviate sample imbalance, and incorporates a curriculum learning strategy for progressive training from simple to complex cases. Experiments on a public dataset demonstrate that PAMBert significantly outperforms traditional baseline models. Quantitatively, on the binary classification task (Task 1), our method achieves a macro F1-score of 0.9923 and an accuracy of 99.22%, representing a 2.3% improvement over the Transformer baseline. On the more challenging multi-class task (Task 2), PAMBert attains a macro F1-score of 0.9752, which is a 5.2% increase compared to the baseline. Ablation studies further confirm that each proposed component contributes to an overall performance gain of up to $\mathbf{2. 5 \%}$ on the complex task. This work provides effective technical support for building a safe and trustworthy interactive environment for large models.},
  keywords={Modeling;Learning (artificial intelligence);Signal detection;Training;Timing;Multitasking;Equations;Printing;Data augmentation;Attention mechanisms;Prompt Injection Detection;Adaptive Multi-task Learning;BERT;Curriculum Learning;Dynamic Loss Weighting},
  doi={10.1109/CCAI69603.2026.11641989},
