  title={Agentic AI Security in IoMT: A PRISMA-Guided Scoping Review and Layered Threat–Defense Taxonomy}, 
  year={2026},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={991-996},
  abstract={The Internet of Medical Things (IoMT) enables continuous patient monitoring and clinical connectivity but substantially expands the healthcare cybersecurity attack surface. AI/ML is frequently proposed as a defense mechanism in IoMT literature, addressing intrusion detection, anomaly detection, authentication, privacy, and secure communication. Emerging Agentic AI integration may introduce a distinct risk layer through external retrieval, long-term memory, dynamic tool use, prompt-driven behavior, autonomous action, and multi-agent coordination. Because direct empirical evidence from live Agentic IoMT deployments remains limited, we conducted a PRISMA-ScR-guided scoping review to map anticipated vulnerabilities. The final corpus contained 19 academic sources plus four standards, frameworks, and guidance documents identified through a parallel targeted search, totaling 23 core sources. Findings indicate that AI/ML is primarily positioned defensively in IoMT literature, whereas agentic capabilities introduce transferred risks from broader LLM-agent security domains. We contribute a seven-layer threat–defense taxonomy and a focused research agenda for empirical validation rather than claims of real-world attack prevalence.},
  keywords={Artificial intelligence;Modeling;Tools;Reviews;Security;Large language models;Printing;Internet of Things;Memory;Medical services;Internet of Medical Things;agentic AI;cybersecurity;LLM agents;prompt injection;PRISMA-ScR;threat modeling;medical-device security},
